            <ArticleTitle>Allele-specific targeting of microRNAs to HLA-G and risk of asthma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>829-34</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>HLA-G is a nonclassic, class I HLA molecule that has important immunomodulatory properties. Previously, we identified HLA-G as an asthma-susceptibility gene and discovered that the risk of asthma in a child was determined by both the child's HLA-G genotype and the mother's affection status. Here we report a SNP in the 3' untranslated region of HLA-G that influences the targeting of three microRNAs (miRNAs) to this gene, and we suggest that allele-specific targeting of these miRNAs accounts, at least in part, for our earlier observations on HLA-G and the risk of asthma.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
